findings.body:
This always sets `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` to the caller-supplied `origin` (or `*`) while also sending `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`.

**Failure mode:** any malicious site can make a credentialed cross-origin request to this API; the browser will attach cookies and expose the response body because ACAO echoes that site's origin. That removes the previous allowlist gate on `https://app.example.com`.

Native mobile HTTP clients do not enforce CORS, so they did not need this change. Mobile webviews that need access should be added to an explicit allowlist (or a tight origin pattern), with credentials emitted only for matched origins.

Also, `ACAO: *` + `Credentials: true` is invalid per the CORS spec when `origin` is null — browsers reject it — so the null-origin branch does not help credentialed clients either.

```suggestion
  const ALLOWED = new Set(["https://app.example.com"]);
  // Extend ALLOWED with known mobile-webview origins rather than reflecting arbitrary Origin.
  if (origin && ALLOWED.has(origin)) {
    return {
      "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": origin,
      "Access-Control-Allow-Credentials": "true",
      Vary: "Origin",
    };
  }
  return { Vary: "Origin" };
```
